# Flutter — ListView

## 1. What is `ListView`?

`ListView` displays multiple widgets in a **scrollable list**.

Example:

```text
Apple
Banana
Mango
Orange
Grapes
   ↓
Scroll
```

Without `ListView`, a long `Column` can cause a **bottom overflow**.

---

# 2. Basic ListView

```dart id="7j5t8p"
ListView(
  children: [

    Text("Apple"),
    Text("Banana"),
    Text("Mango"),
    Text("Orange"),

  ],
)
```

The user can scroll through the items.

---

# 3. ListView + Card

A very common real-world pattern:

```dart id="v3f6zq"
ListView(
  children: [

    Card(
      child: ListTile(
        title: Text("Apple"),
        subtitle: Text("₹100"),
      ),
    ),

    Card(
      child: ListTile(
        title: Text("Banana"),
        subtitle: Text("₹50"),
      ),
    ),

  ],
)
```

---

# 4. `ListView.builder`

When the list contains **many or dynamic items**, use:

```dart id="k6k5qw"
ListView.builder(
  itemCount: 10,

  itemBuilder: (context, index) {

    return Text("Item $index");

  },
)
```

Output conceptually:

```text
Item 0
Item 1
Item 2
Item 3
Item 4
...
Item 9
```

### How it works

```text id="e4d5mm"
itemCount
    ↓
How many items?

itemBuilder
    ↓
How should each item look?

index
    ↓
Which item is being created?
```

---

# 5. Why `builder`?

Suppose you have 10,000 products.

Instead of manually writing:

```dart
Text("Product 1"),
Text("Product 2"),
Text("Product 3"),
...
```

you use:

```dart id="v2f1ub"
ListView.builder(
  itemCount: products.length,

  itemBuilder: (context, index) {
    return Text(products[index]);
  },
)
```

This is better for dynamic lists.

---

# 6. Using a List

Suppose:

```dart id="t7j0so"
List<String> fruits = [
  "Apple",
  "Banana",
  "Mango",
  "Orange",
];
```

Then:

```dart id="6o1w0s"
ListView.builder(
  itemCount: fruits.length,

  itemBuilder: (context, index) {

    return ListTile(
      title: Text(fruits[index]),
    );

  },
)
```

Output:

```text
Apple
Banana
Mango
Orange
```

---

# 7. `index`

`index` tells you the position of the current item.

```dart id="4l5qpy"
itemBuilder: (context, index) {

  print(index);

  return Text(fruits[index]);
}
```

For the list:

```text
Apple
Banana
Mango
```

indexes are:

```text
Apple   → 0
Banana  → 1
Mango   → 2
```

Remember:

**Python lists and Dart lists both use zero-based indexing.**

---

# 8. ListView with `ListTile`

`ListTile` is commonly used for simple list rows.

```dart id="n3v2zz"
ListTile(
  leading: Icon(Icons.person),

  title: Text("Rahul"),

  subtitle: Text("Software Developer"),

  trailing: Icon(Icons.arrow_forward_ios),

  onTap: () {
    print("Rahul clicked");
  },
)
```

Structure:

```text id="xk2q9f"
┌─────────────────────────────────┐
│ 👤  Rahul              >        │
│     Software Developer          │
└─────────────────────────────────┘
```

### Important properties

```text id="4ghn5c"
leading   → left side
title     → main text
subtitle  → secondary text
trailing  → right side
onTap     → click action
```

---

# 9. Real Example — Product List

```dart id="w4y5z2"
List<String> products = [
  "Laptop",
  "Phone",
  "Headphones",
  "Keyboard",
];

ListView.builder(
  itemCount: products.length,

  itemBuilder: (context, index) {

    return Card(
      child: ListTile(

        leading: Icon(Icons.shopping_bag),

        title: Text(products[index]),

        subtitle: Text("Available"),

        trailing: Icon(Icons.arrow_forward_ios),

        onTap: () {
          print(products[index]);
        },
      ),
    );
  },
)
```

---

# 10. `ListView.separated`

If you want a separator between items:

```dart id="3c5s6m"
ListView.separated(

  itemCount: fruits.length,

  itemBuilder: (context, index) {
    return ListTile(
      title: Text(fruits[index]),
    );
  },

  separatorBuilder: (context, index) {
    return Divider();
  },
)
```

Conceptually:

```text
Apple
────────────
Banana
────────────
Mango
────────────
Orange
```

---

# 11. Horizontal ListView

By default, `ListView` scrolls vertically.

For horizontal scrolling:

```dart id="8b4h0k"
ListView.builder(

  scrollDirection: Axis.horizontal,

  itemCount: 10,

  itemBuilder: (context, index) {

    return Container(
      width: 150,
      margin: EdgeInsets.all(8),
      child: Card(
        child: Center(
          child: Text("Item $index"),
        ),
      ),
    );
  },
)
```

Useful for:

* Categories
* Movies
* Product recommendations
* Stories
* Horizontal cards

---

# 12. ListView vs Column

### Column

```dart id="5v6d7a"
Column(
  children: [
    Text("A"),
    Text("B"),
    Text("C"),
  ],
)
```

Column does **not automatically scroll**.

### ListView

```dart id="7m2k6p"
ListView(
  children: [
    Text("A"),
    Text("B"),
    Text("C"),
  ],
)
```

ListView **scrolls**.

### Easy rule

```text id="y7r3mw"
Few items + no scrolling
        ↓
      Column

Many items / scrolling
        ↓
     ListView
```

---

# 13. Important Warning: ListView inside Column

This can cause layout problems:

```dart id="j9l6a0"
Column(
  children: [

    Text("Products"),

    ListView.builder(
      itemCount: 20,
      itemBuilder: (context, index) {
        return Text("Product");
      },
    ),

  ],
)
```

Why?

The `Column` doesn't give the `ListView` a definite height.

Use `Expanded`:

```dart id="m0v8dd"
Column(
  children: [

    Text("Products"),

    Expanded(
      child: ListView.builder(
        itemCount: 20,

        itemBuilder: (context, index) {
          return Text("Product");
        },
      ),
    ),

  ],
)
```

Now:

```text
Column
 │
 ├── Text
 │
 └── Expanded
       │
       └── ListView
```

`Expanded` gives the ListView the remaining screen space.

---

# 14. Most Important ListView Syntax

### Simple list

```dart id="5f3c8a"
ListView(
  children: [
    Text("A"),
    Text("B"),
    Text("C"),
  ],
)
```

### Dynamic list

```dart id="v9n1xk"
ListView.builder(
  itemCount: items.length,

  itemBuilder: (context, index) {
    return Text(items[index]);
  },
)
```

### List with separators

```dart id="7c1d2r"
ListView.separated(
  itemCount: items.length,

  itemBuilder: (context, index) {
    return Text(items[index]);
  },

  separatorBuilder: (context, index) {
    return Divider();
  },
)
```

---

## Mental Model

```text
ListView
   ↓
Scrollable container
   ↓
Many items
   ↓
builder creates each item
   ↓
index identifies the item
```

## One-line revision

**`ListView` creates a scrollable list, and `ListView.builder` is used when the list contains dynamic or many items.**





Example ;- 

# Dynamic ListView.builder — Image + Title + Small Info

## 1. Data

First create a list containing the data for each card:

```dart
List<Map<String, String>> products = [
  {
    "image": "https://picsum.photos/id/1/200",
    "title": "Laptop",
    "info": "Powerful laptop for work",
  },
  {
    "image": "https://picsum.photos/id/2/200",
    "title": "Headphones",
    "info": "Wireless noise cancelling",
  },
  {
    "image": "https://picsum.photos/id/3/200",
    "title": "Smartphone",
    "info": "5G smartphone with AMOLED display",
  },
  {
    "image": "https://picsum.photos/id/4/200",
    "title": "Smart Watch",
    "info": "Fitness and health tracking",
  },
];
```

The important idea is:

```text
products
   ↓
List of multiple data
   ↓
ListView.builder
   ↓
One Card for each data item
```

---

## 2. Complete Dynamic Code

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

void main() {
  runApp(
    MaterialApp(
      debugShowCheckedModeBanner: false,
      home: ProductPage(),
    ),
  );
}

class ProductPage extends StatelessWidget {
  ProductPage({super.key});

  final List<Map<String, String>> products = [
    {
      "image": "https://picsum.photos/id/1/200",
      "title": "Laptop",
      "info": "Powerful laptop for work",
    },
    {
      "image": "https://picsum.photos/id/2/200",
      "title": "Headphones",
      "info": "Wireless noise cancelling",
    },
    {
      "image": "https://picsum.photos/id/3/200",
      "title": "Smartphone",
      "info": "5G smartphone with AMOLED display",
    },
    {
      "image": "https://picsum.photos/id/4/200",
      "title": "Smart Watch",
      "info": "Fitness and health tracking",
    },
  ];

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Scaffold(
      appBar: AppBar(
        title: Text("Products"),
      ),

      body: ListView.builder(
        padding: EdgeInsets.all(10),

        itemCount: products.length,

        itemBuilder: (context, index) {

          final product = products[index];

          return Card(
            margin: EdgeInsets.only(bottom: 10),

            child: Padding(
              padding: EdgeInsets.all(10),

              child: Row(
                children: [

                  // IMAGE
                  ClipRRect(
                    borderRadius: BorderRadius.circular(10),

                    child: Image.network(
                      product["image"]!,
                      width: 80,
                      height: 80,
                      fit: BoxFit.cover,
                    ),
                  ),

                  SizedBox(width: 15),

                  // TITLE + INFO
                  Expanded(
                    child: Column(
                      crossAxisAlignment: CrossAxisAlignment.start,

                      children: [

                        Text(
                          product["title"]!,
                          style: TextStyle(
                            fontSize: 18,
                            fontWeight: FontWeight.bold,
                          ),
                        ),

                        SizedBox(height: 5),

                        Text(
                          product["info"]!,
                          style: TextStyle(
                            fontSize: 14,
                            color: Colors.grey,
                          ),
                        ),
                      ],
                    ),
                  ),
                ],
              ),
            ),
          );
        },
      ),
    );
  }
}
```

## 3. How the Dynamic Part Works

This is the most important part:

```dart
final product = products[index];
```

Suppose:

```text
index = 0
```

Then:

```dart
product
```

contains:

```text
image → laptop image
title → Laptop
info  → Powerful laptop for work
```

When:

```text
index = 1
```

it automatically gets:

```text
image → headphones image
title → Headphones
info  → Wireless noise cancelling
```

So **you don't manually create four Cards**.

---

## 4. The Important Dynamic Lines

### Number of cards

```dart
itemCount: products.length,
```

If you add 10 products → 10 cards.

If you add 100 products → 100 cards.

---

### Get current data

```dart
final product = products[index];
```

---

### Get image

```dart
product["image"]!
```

### Get title

```dart
product["title"]!
```

### Get small information

```dart
product["info"]!
```

---

## 5. Visual Structure

Each item is basically:

```text
Card
 │
 └── Padding
      │
      └── Row
           │
           ├── Image
           │
           ├── SizedBox
           │
           └── Expanded
                │
                └── Column
                     ├── Title
                     └── Small Info
```

Result:

```text
┌──────────────────────────────────┐
│  ┌──────┐                        │
│  │ IMG  │  Laptop                │
│  │      │  Powerful laptop...    │
│  └──────┘                        │
└──────────────────────────────────┘

┌──────────────────────────────────┐
│  ┌──────┐                        │
│  │ IMG  │  Headphones            │
│  │      │  Wireless noise...     │
│  └──────┘                        │
└──────────────────────────────────┘
```

## 6. Why `Expanded`?

We use:

```dart
Expanded(
  child: Column(...)
)
```

because the image takes some width and the text needs to use the **remaining space**.

It also helps prevent horizontal overflow when the title/info becomes long.

## 7. The Pattern to Remember

For most dynamic cards, remember this pattern:

```dart
ListView.builder(
  itemCount: data.length,

  itemBuilder: (context, index) {

    final item = data[index];

    return Card(
      child: Row(
        children: [

          Image.network(item["image"]!),

          Expanded(
            child: Column(
              children: [
                Text(item["title"]!),
                Text(item["info"]!),
              ],
            ),
          ),

        ],
      ),
    );
  },
)
```

This same pattern can be used for:

* Products
* Songs
* Users
* News
* Movies
* Courses
* Restaurants
* Contacts
* Messages

The **UI stays the same; only the data changes**.
